# dot_construction.tkr — the §4.1 `.{ … }` target-typed construction ACCEPTANCE channel. `.{ … }`
# builds the CONTEXT's expected type; each scenario's probe answers a BOOL that only the correctly
# targeted construction makes true, announced by name. TEST = the printed token, never the exit code
# (the axis law). The NOMINAL `Name { … }` form coexists and is checked here too (nominal_still_ok).

Feature: §4.1 `.{ … }` target-typed construction builds the context's expected type

  # (dot_static_self_ok) a trailing `.{ … }` in a `static fn … : self` builds the canonical `self`
  # return target — the exact namespaced case the retired `self { … }` construct could not widen.
  Scenario: dot_static_self_ok — a trailing `.{ … }` builds the `self` return
    When built and run
    Then stdout pattern = "scenario dot_static_self_ok: ok"

  # (dot_return_stmt) `return .{ … }` builds the `self` return target.
  Scenario: dot_return_stmt — `return .{ … }` builds the return target
    When built and run
    Then stdout pattern = "scenario dot_return_stmt: ok"

  # (dot_binding_annotated) an annotated binding `var x: Counter = .{ … }` builds the annotation.
  Scenario: dot_binding_annotated — an annotated binding builds its declared type
    When built and run
    Then stdout pattern = "scenario dot_binding_annotated: ok"

  # (dot_arg) a `.{ … }` argument builds the parameter type.
  Scenario: dot_arg — a `.{ … }` argument builds the parameter type
    When built and run
    Then stdout pattern = "scenario dot_arg: ok"

  # (dot_field_nested) a nested field value `.{ … }` builds the field type.
  Scenario: dot_field_nested — a nested field value builds the field type
    When built and run
    Then stdout pattern = "scenario dot_field_nested: ok"

  # (dot_generic_self) a generic self-return `.{ … }` phantom-stamps per concrete width.
  Scenario: dot_generic_self — a generic self-return builds the phantom instance per width
    When built and run
    Then stdout pattern = "scenario dot_generic_self: ok"

  # (nominal_still_ok) the NOMINAL `Name { … }` form (with and without type-args) coexists with
  # `.{ … }` and still constructs (§4.1 — the two forms are complementary).
  Scenario: nominal_still_ok — nominal `Name { … }` construction still compiles and runs
    When built and run
    Then stdout pattern = "scenario nominal_still_ok: ok"
